target: internal/push/push.go
verify: go test ./internal/push/ -count=1 -run 'TestCheckDiagnosesARunMissingFromTheStore'
expect: want the empty-record refusal ahead of the
why:    ordering, which is behaviour here because Decide returns on the FIRST failing check. Moving the empty-record block below the report and branch checks leaves it reachable only when all of those pass, so the common cases — a mistyped -run-id against a repo with no landed report — refuse with "run pr-report.py --post first", an instruction that cannot fix them, and the operator never learns the record was empty. The refusal is correct either way, which is what makes the ordering silent: only the REASON changes, and the reason is the whole operator-facing value of this gate.
--- old
	if s.NoRecord {
		return false, "no readable record for this run — convergence, the outcome and the " +
			"report fingerprint would all come off an empty record; check -store and -run-id"
	}
	for _, bad := range BrokenOutcomes {
--- new
	for _, bad := range BrokenOutcomes {
